I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 5 of 943 (part 4 of 7).\par\par\par
The Sunday I Sat On The Bathroom Floor With The App Open\par\par\par
It was a Sunday in early October, around 9:40 PM, and I was sitting on the bathroom floor with my back against the tub.\par\par\par
It was a Sunday in early October, around 9:40 PM, and I was sitting on the bathroom floor with my back against the tub.\par\par\par
I want to be clear that nothing dramatic happened. Nobody found me. I wasn't crying. I wasn't drunk. There was no rock bottom in the movie sense \emdash  no phone call, no confrontation, no letter left on the counter. It was just a Sunday. My dog was in the hallway watching me through the doorway with the specific expression dogs get when they don't understand why you're not in the bedroom.\par\par\par
What had happened was this. At 8:15 PM I had placed a bet for $3,100 on a market I'd been watching for two weeks. It was a big one. It was, in my mind, the bet that was going to reset the numbers. I had done everything right this time. I had waited for the full market to open. I had checked both platforms. They agreed within two cents. I had watched the volume come in. I had watched the sharps come in \emdash  or what I thought were the sharps, which was volume moving in ways I had decided were significant based on nothing but the fact that I wanted them to be significant.\par\par\par
I bought at 58.\par\par\par
The market resolved at 9:37 PM. It resolved at zero.\par\par\par
At 9:38 PM my account balance was $1,204.\par\par\par
I had started the arc six weeks earlier with $11,000 that I did not, technically, have a use for. It had been sitting in a savings account earning 4.2% interest. It was money I had promised myself was for a specific thing \emdash  a bathroom renovation, the very bathroom I was now sitting on the floor of, which had a crack running down the tile behind the toilet that I looked at every morning while brushing my teeth and had been looking at for two years.\par\par\par
$11,000 in. $1,204 out. Six weeks.\par\par\par
I sat down on the floor because I was in the bathroom when I looked at the number and I could not think of a reason to stand up.\par\par\par
My dog stared at me. I stared back. The app was still open on my phone in my hand. The screen kept dimming and I kept tapping it to keep it lit, because the moment it went dark I knew I was going to have to think about something other than the app and I was not ready to think about anything other than the app.\par\par\par
Here is what went through my head, in the order I remember it going through:\par\par\par
One, this is fine. There is $1,204 left, and I've come back from worse spots than this in my life. That was the first thought. That thought lasted about eleven seconds.\par\par\par
Two, I could take the $1,204 and put it on something with 8-to-1 odds and if it hit I'd be back to $9,600 and no one would ever have to know. This thought lasted longer. Maybe two minutes. I actually opened a market. I actually put my finger on the amount field. I did not enter a number, but I thought about the number. The number I thought about was $800.\par\par\par
Three, I do not know who I am anymore. That was the third thought, and it arrived without any of the melodrama I'm making it sound like it had. It was flat. Almost bored. I had spent six weeks being a guy who was going to figure this out, and in the space of ninety seconds that guy had evaporated, and what was left was a 55-year-old man on a bathroom floor with a cracked tile and a dog and a phone.\par\par\par
Four, and this is the one that made me finally put the phone down: I remembered my father. My father was not a gambler. My father was a Wednesday-night bowling league man with a pension and a Buick. But my father, once, at a family reunion when I was 14, had told me that the trick to not being a loser was not to be a loser. That was the whole speech. I remember the exact phrasing because it made no sense to me at 14 and made no sense to me for forty years and then, on that bathroom floor at 9:47 PM, made complete sense. He wasn't talking about outcomes. He was talking about identity. He was saying: don't let it become who you are.\par\par\par
It had become who I was. Six weeks. That's all it took.\par\par\par
I put the phone face down on the tile. My dog came into the bathroom and lay down next to me, which is something she does not normally do because the tile is cold. She stayed there for what turned out to be forty minutes. I know because when I finally stood up the clock said 10:27.\par\par\par
I did not sleep that night. I did not place another bet. Those are two separate accomplishments. The second one was harder than the first.\par\par\par
Next: the crawl out, including the two lies I told my brother and the one thing I couldn't delete.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
